---
name: ptt-note
description: "Write and record a note from your topic and gist, then show where to carry it. Argument: designer, integrator, or an open task's ID."
disable-model-invocation: true
---

# Note from the user

The command, or an AtE naming these records, approves the note this skill
writes; the work it proposes still needs AtE (rules file). This is a
user-invoked command, not a step for sessions to invoke themselves.

1. From the user's topic, gist, and conversation, write the note in your
   own words. Ask only if the recipient or intended message is missing.
   Preserve the distinction between a proposal and an agreed decision.
   Write `<records>/handoff/notes/<topic>.md`, choosing an unused filename
   (add a suffix if needed), with front matter:

   ```markdown
   ---
   to: <designer|integrator|T0007>
   ---
   ```

   Use the requested recipient; `ptt record` validates it. A correction
   goes in a new note identifying the earlier one. Leave the earlier note
   in place for its recipient.
2. Record at once with `<records>/bin/ptt record -m "note: <topic>"
   <records>/handoff/notes/<topic>.md`, as this session's holder if it has
   one. Show no draft or approval question first. If recording is refused,
   report the tool's error and that the note is not recorded; stop.
3. Follow the tool's `nudges:` instructions for any `nudge:` targets.
   `queued:` means ptt already enqueued a Codex notice: report acceptance,
   with delivery unconfirmed. Report queue errors and needed sandbox
   approvals; do not repeat the recording command. Pass each `carry:` line
   to the user; for a Claude Code target absent from
   `ListAgents`, give the manual line as instructed. Before naming a current
   holder, run `<records>/bin/ptt who` (rules file). End with only the
   transfer lines needed, no summary of the note. When every target is
   nudged or queued, no manual transfer line is needed. If the recipient
   has no holder, say so; the note waits in its inbox when someone takes it.
